WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.902 --> 00:00:18.694
My plunge into the sea was so unexpected that I can't remember what I was thinking at the time.

2
00:00:18.695 --> 00:00:21.813
I'm a good swimmer, so I know I didn't panic.

3
00:00:21.814 --> 00:00:26.226
Once I was back at the surface, my first thought was to look for my ship.

4
00:00:26.227 --> 00:00:33.043
Had the crew seen me fall? Had the Abraham Lincoln turned around? Did I have any hope of being saved?

5
00:00:33.044 --> 00:00:41.630
I caught a glimpse of a ship disappearing in the east, its lights dying out in the distance. It was the Abraham Lincoln! I was doomed.

6
00:00:41.631 --> 00:00:44.702
"Help! Help!" I shouted.

7
00:00:44.703 --> 00:00:52.367
I swam desperately toward the ship, but my clothes made it difficult to move. I was sinking . . . I was suffocating!

8
00:00:52.368 --> 00:00:53.948
"Help!"

9
00:00:53.949 --> 00:00:58.099
That was my last cry before I started to slip below the waves.

10
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:04.361
Suddenly a strong hand seized my clothes, and I felt myself being pulled to the surface.

11
00:01:04.362 --> 00:01:07.495
Then I heard these words in my ear:

12
00:01:07.496 --> 00:01:12.678
"If you would kindly lean on my shoulder, you'll be able to swim more easily."

13
00:01:12.679 --> 00:01:19.345
"Conseil!" I cried as I seized my faithful servant's arm. "Is that really you?"

14
00:01:19.346 --> 00:01:23.678
"Yes, I'm here awaiting your orders," replied Conseil.

15
00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:26.829
"That horrible collision threw you into the sea too?"

16
00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:30.995
"No, but being your servant, I naturally followed you into the water."

17
00:01:30.996 --> 00:01:33.916
"And what about the ship?" I asked.

18
00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:39.511
Conseil flipped over to float on his back. "I don't think you should count on that ship to rescue you.

19
00:01:39.512 --> 00:01:45.670
When I threw myself into the water, I heard the man at the wheel say that both the rudder and propeller were broken."

20
00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:46.987
"Broken?"

21
00:01:46.988 --> 00:01:49.605
"Yes, broken by the monster's teeth.

22
00:01:49.606 --> 00:01:56.392
That's the only damage to the Abraham Lincoln, but it's bad news for us because they can't steer the ship anymore."

23
00:01:56.393 --> 00:01:58.987
"Then we are lost!" I cried.

24
00:01:58.988 --> 00:02:02.306
"Perhaps," Conseil replied calmly.

25
00:02:02.306 --> 00:02:08.638
"But we can survive for at least several hours more, and one can do a lot in several hours."

26
00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:14.051
Conseil's unruffled calmness encouraged me, and I swam more vigorously.

27
00:02:14.052 --> 00:02:22.297
Since the Abraham Lincoln couldn't turn back to rescue us, our only hope was that Captain Farragut had sent out the small boats to search for us.

28
00:02:22.298 --> 00:02:25.543
We vowed to try to wait for as long as possible.

29
00:02:25.544 --> 00:02:31.622
I decided that we should conserve our strength so that the two of us wouldn't be exhausted at the same time.

30
00:02:31.623 --> 00:02:40.193
Here's how we managed this: While one man floated on his back, the other man swam and pushed the floating man ahead of him.

31
00:02:40.194 --> 00:02:46.019
Taking turns for ten minutes at a time, we could swim for hours, perhaps until daybreak.

32
00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.868
The Abraham Lincoln's collision with the creature had taken place around eleven o'clock at night.

33
00:02:50.869 --> 00:02:57.765
I reckoned that we had eight hours to swim before sunrise, which we could do if we kept relieving each other.

34
00:02:57.766 --> 00:03:01.098
The sea was very calm, so that was in our favor.

35
00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:05.670
But around one o'clock in the morning, I was overcome with terrible fatigue.

36
00:03:05.671 --> 00:03:13.924
My arms and legs were seized with horrible cramps. Conseil had to keep me afloat and our survival depended on him alone.

37
00:03:13.925 --> 00:03:19.583
I heard the poor fellow panting and I felt that he couldn't keep up this effort much longer.

38
00:03:19.584 --> 00:03:22.075
"Leave me!" I said to him.

39
00:03:22.076 --> 00:03:26.908
"Leave my master? Never," he replied. "I would rather drown."

40
00:03:26.909 --> 00:03:34.980
Just then the moon appeared at the edge of a thick cloud. The surface of the sea glittered with its rays, and we felt revived.

41
00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:38.630
I scanned the horizon and spotted the Abraham Lincoln!

42
00:03:38.631 --> 00:03:44.471
But there were no small boats on the water, and the ship appeared to be about five miles away.

43
00:03:44.472 --> 00:03:52.471
My swollen lips could make no sound. Conseil could still speak, and periodically I heard him cry out for help.

44
00:03:52.472 --> 00:03:55.217
We stopped moving for a moment and listened.

45
00:03:55.218 --> 00:03:59.789
"Did you hear that?" I murmured. "It sounded like someone answered you."

46
00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:05.281
"Yes! Yes!" said Conseil, and he gave one more despairing call.

47
00:04:05.282 --> 00:04:10.472
This time there was no mistake; a human voice had responded to Conseil's call.

48
00:04:10.473 --> 00:04:17.797
Was it another unfortunate victim who had fallen from the ship? Or was it a small boat hailing us in the darkness?

49
00:04:17.798 --> 00:04:23.915
Leaning on my shoulder, Conseil raised himself half out of the water and then fell back exhausted.

50
00:04:23.916 --> 00:04:26.289
"What did you see?" I asked.

51
00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:33.368
"I saw . . . " he whispered. "I saw . . . But we shouldn't talk. We should save our strength."

52
00:04:33.369 --> 00:04:36.868
Conseil was once more propelling me through the water.

53
00:04:36.869 --> 00:04:45.431
Sometimes he raised his head and uttered a cry that was answered by a voice that came nearer and nearer. I barely heard it.

54
00:04:45.432 --> 00:04:50.185
My strength was exhausted; my mouth opened and filled with salt water.

55
00:04:50.186 --> 00:04:54.670
I raised my head for the last time, and then I sank below the surface.

56
00:04:54.671 --> 00:05:01.678
At this moment I struck a hard body. I clung to it, and then felt that I was being pulled up.

57
00:05:01.679 --> 00:05:06.837
I fainted. But I quickly came to because someone was trying to revive me.

58
00:05:06.838 --> 00:05:10.488
"Conseil," I murmured as I half opened my eyes.

59
00:05:10.489 --> 00:05:13.734
"Did you call me, Master?" asked Conseil.

60
00:05:13.735 --> 00:05:19.400
But it was not Conseil who was vigorously rubbing my arms and legs. It was Ned Land.

61
00:05:19.401 --> 00:05:24.630
"Ned!" I cried. "Were you thrown into the sea by the shock of the collision?"

62
00:05:24.631 --> 00:05:34.305
"Yes, but fortunately I was able to quickly climb onto this island, or, perhaps I should say, climb onto our narwhal," replied the harpooner.

63
00:05:34.306 --> 00:05:38.963
"I soon discovered why my harpoon couldn't penetrate its skin."

64
00:05:38.964 --> 00:05:43.217
"Why, Ned?" I said, thoroughly puzzled by this conversation.

65
00:05:43.218 --> 00:05:47.971
"Because, Professor, the beast is made of sheet metal."

66
00:05:47.972 --> 00:05:53.456
I sat up. The three of us were resting on top of something that was smooth and black.

67
00:05:53.457 --> 00:05:56.955
Most of it was submerged below the surface of the water.

68
00:05:56.956 --> 00:06:03.868
I kicked my heels against our creature and heard a metallic sound in return. There was no doubt about it.

69
00:06:03.869 --> 00:06:10.598
This monster, this creature, this animal that had puzzled the world was man-made.

70
00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:17.098
Ned was convinced that we were lying on the back of a submarine, and Conseil and I could only agree with him.

71
00:06:17.099 --> 00:06:25.107
Just then a bubbling began at the back of this strange thing, and the vessel began to move. We hung on for dear life.

72
00:06:25.108 --> 00:06:33.328
"As long as it sails horizontally, I don't mind," muttered Ned. "But if it decides to dive, we're in trouble."

73
00:06:33.329 --> 00:06:39.495
This long night continued to pass slowly. We clung to the strange vessel as it picked up speed.

74
00:06:39.496 --> 00:06:44.011
Several times I thought I heard some vague sounds coming from below us.

75
00:06:44.012 --> 00:06:49.082
It was almost as if someone had given a command and voices were responding.

76
00:06:49.083 --> 00:06:56.472
What kind of beings existed in this strange boat? I wondered. What was the source of its great speed?

77
00:06:56.473 --> 00:07:04.225
Daybreak finally appeared. I was about to examine the hull upon which we rested when I felt the whole vessel starting to sink.

78
00:07:04.226 --> 00:07:11.384
"Oh!" cried Ned, furiously kicking the metal beneath his feet. "Open up, you inhospitable rascals!"

79
00:07:11.385 --> 00:07:16.304
The sinking movement stopped at once. We heard the sound of metal moving.

80
00:07:16.305 --> 00:07:23.647
An iron plate slid aside and a man popped up. He uttered a strange cry and immediately disappeared.

81
00:07:23.648 --> 00:07:32.362
Moments later eight masked men appeared and silently forced the three of us into their strange machine.
